    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **53 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp39Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the tenure-based membership-level rule in `Owner.getMembershipLevel()` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`).\n\n**The model:** membership level derives on read from three factors \u2014\n- base `1`\n- `+1` when an email is present\n- `+1` when `namesakeCount` is `0`\n- `+1` when tenure exceeds 365 days (whole days between `registrationDate` and the current date)\n\ncapped at 4.\n\n**Why this is correct:** I checked `Cp23Tests`, which asserts that the third member of a 3-member household (no email, unique first name so `namesakeCount == 0`, `householdMemberCount == 3`) is level **2**. That rules out `householdMemberCount` being a level factor \u2014 so the \"3-member household\" in the spec is just describing the scenario, not a scoring input. The 4th level comes from tenure itself. A newly created owner is registered on the current date, so its tenure is zero, it never gets the tenure `+1`, and a maxed-out new owner (email + `namesakeCount 0`) lands at exactly **level 3, not 4** \u2014 satisfying the spec and the new `Cp39Tests`, while leaving all prior tests green.\n\nI also updated the method's Javadoc to describe the tenure rule and the 1-to-4 range, and added the `java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit` import used for the day count.",
